Phase 6, fourth slice: a real bug in the per-request JSON-schema mechanism
(evidence 48), found by reading llama.cpp's own server documentation
against the real, pinned b10948 build before trusting an assumption
carried over from OpenAI's API shape.

## 1. The bug

`ai/eval/run/main.go`'s `responseFormat` (built in evidence 48 to carry
`jsonSchemaFor`'s per-case schema, replacing the server-wide `-jf`
startup flag so the "evidence_handles" enum and confidence-ceiling
maximum could vary case to case) was shaped like OpenAI's
`response_format` field: `{"type": "json_schema", "json_schema": {"name":
..., "schema": {...}}}` - a schema nested two levels deep under a
"json_schema" wrapper object.

Checked against `tools/server/README.md` at the exact commit b10948 was
built from (`5f436dddb440a288ee5611d7d1eca564a6aca9f4`, the commit this
project has already pinned) rather than assumed: llama-server's own
`response_format` parameter puts the schema directly at the top level -
`{"type": "json_schema", "schema": {...}}`, no intermediate wrapper. This
would not have errored: llama-server would simply not find a "schema" key
at the top level of the object it actually looks at, and would silently
apply no constraint at all - every guardrail evidence 48/49 built
(evidence-handle enum, confidence-ceiling maximum) would have done
nothing against a real server, with no failure anywhere to notice it by.
Caught before a single real request was sent to the actual downloaded
model, while preparing the live benchmark run on the second test machine
(see evidence 48 §1 and §8 for why that machine, not this one).

## 2. The fix

`responseFormat` now marshals to exactly `{"type": "json_schema",
"schema": {...}}` - the `jsonSchemaBody` wrapper type is gone entirely,
not left as unused dead code. The doc comment above `jsonSchemaFor` (which
had also stated the wrong OpenAI-shaped path) is corrected to point at
`responseFormat`'s own comment, which cites the exact README section and
pinned commit this was checked against.

## 3. Full regression

    $ go build ./...                               clean
    $ go vet ./...                                  clean
    $ CGO_ENABLED=0 go test ./...                   every package passes
    $ gofmt -l ai/eval/                             clean

## 4. Proven, not just passing once - a stronger proof than usual

`TestResponseFormatMatchesLlamaServersOwnShapeNotOpenAIs` (new): marshals
a `responseFormat` and asserts the schema is reachable at the top-level
`"schema"` key and that no `"json_schema"` wrapper key exists at all.

The deliberate-break proof here caught the regression a level earlier
than the usual runtime-assertion-fails pattern: reverting `responseFormat`
to the wrapped OpenAI shape (and fixing up the one production call site
to match, so the package would build) made the TEST FILE ITSELF fail to
compile - both this new test and the pre-existing
`TestChatCompletionRequestSendsThePerCaseSchema` construct a
`responseFormat{..., Schema: ...}` literal directly, which is simply not
a valid field on the wrapped type. A compile failure is a stronger
guarantee than a passing-then-failing runtime assertion: it is not
possible to even build a version of this package where the test
compiles against the wrong shape, let alone accidentally ship one.
Restored immediately after, confirmed all tests pass again from a clean
build.

## 5. Why this was caught now rather than earlier

Evidence 48 built and unit-tested `jsonSchemaFor`'s output shape and
`chatComplete`'s request construction entirely against Go's own type
system and `encoding/json` - correct as far as it went, but nothing in
that slice's own tests checked the marshaled request against llama-server's
actual documented API, because no real server existed to check against
yet in that slice. This is exactly why the standing verification
discipline distinguishes "passes its own tests" from "verified against
the real system": the former was true the whole time; the latter needed
either a real server or, short of that, a direct read of the real
server's own interface documentation - done here specifically because
preparing to run against the real, downloaded model surfaced the need to
double check every assumption the request-building code made, not just
its internal consistency.
